{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "azuresql-db-ci",
  "domain": "azure-sql-database-container",
  "value": [
    "not-in-training-data",
    "corrects-a-wrong-assumption",
    "multi-step-with-silent-failures"
  ],
  "correction": "An agent adds the SQL Server image as a CI service and queries it immediately. This image comes from a private registry that needs credentials as secrets, the health check has to run sqlcmd inside the container because the runner has no client tools, and the user database must exist before the tests do.",
  "posture": [
    "read",
    "write",
    "execute",
    "provision"
  ],
  "maturity": "preview",
  "value_declaration": {
    "rationale": "This skill's kernel is which engine the CI service container is actually running and what readiness means for it. The Azure SQL Database container is a gated private preview and the image is not publicly available, so no model has training data on the product, and the arm of a two-arm value run that ran without this skill would fail for the least interesting reason available: it has never heard of the thing. Running that measurement across all seventeen container skills would spend around a thousand model requests to re-establish what the product's release status already establishes. Carlos Robles accepted value for this collection on 2026-09-04 on this written argument instead. THIS IS A DECLARATION AND NOT A MEASUREMENT, and nothing may report it as one. The argument reaches only the container-specific corrections listed in covers, which are the ones no training data contains. It does not reach GitHub Actions workflow syntax, the services block, job matrices and the shape of a retry loop, which is ordinary material an ordinary model already handles, and whether this skill improves an answer there is unmeasured and stays unmeasured. The argument ends when the Azure SQL Database container reaches Public Preview: training data begins to contain the product, the premise that nothing could know it stops being true, and this skill has to be measured or demoted.",
    "covers": [
      "the service container being the Azure SQL Database engine, EngineEdition 5 with Edition 'SQL Azure', and not the SQL Server image every published example uses",
      "USE being refused in a user-database session with Msg 40508, so the database is selected in the connection string",
      "sqlcmd living at /opt/mssql-tools18/bin/sqlcmd inside the image, which is the path every readiness command in the body depends on",
      "the provisioning session on master being a different session from the one the statement filter is enforced in"
    ],
    "does_not_cover": [
      "GitHub Actions workflow syntax, the services block and job matrices, all abundant in training data",
      "retry with backoff as a pattern, as distinct from what the readiness gate has to poll"
    ],
    "decided_by": "Carlos Robles",
    "decided_on": "2026-09-04",
    "expires_when": "the-container-reaches-public-preview"
  },
  "applies_to": [
    "azure-sql-db-container"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "container",
    "assert": [
      "registry credentials handled as secrets",
      "health check runs inside the container",
      "user database provisioned before tests"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "sql": "SELECT CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('EngineEdition') AS INT) AS engine_edition, CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('Edition') AS NVARCHAR(128)) AS edition;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              5,
              "SQL Azure"
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The interception the skill exists for, made executable. The workflow is only worth writing if the service container is the Azure SQL Database engine rather than the SQL Server image, and these two properties are the only thing that tells them apart from inside a test run. If this ever returns 3 or 'Developer Edition', the pipeline is testing the wrong product and every other claim in this skill is beside the point."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SELECT CAST(CASE WHEN DB_NAME() = 'master' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS INT) AS on_master;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              0
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The skill's rule that tests run against the user database and never master, and the guard that makes the next probe mean something. A session that has landed on master would pass the USE probe below for the wrong reason, because master is a provisioning session where the Azure statement filter is not enforced. This probe fails first and says so."
      },
      {
        "sql": "USE master;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 40508
        },
        "why": "The skill tells a reader to select the database in the connection string rather than switch with USE, and asserts the engine refuses the switch exactly as Azure SQL Database in the cloud does. Microsoft Learn documents error 40508, severity 16, 'USE statement is not supported to switch between databases. Use a new connection to connect to a different database.', and states the refusal applies when a database other than the current one is named, which is why this probe names master rather than the scratch database it is already connected to."
      },
      {
        "where": "container",
        "cmd": "test -x /opt/mssql-tools18/bin/sqlcmd",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "The design the whole workflow rests on: the health check and the provisioning step both invoke sqlcmd at this absolute path inside the engine container, which is what lets the skill promise that the hosted runner needs no client tools of its own. If a future image drops the bundled tools or moves them, the health check never turns healthy and the job hangs until the retry budget runs out, with nothing in the log naming a missing binary. This is deliberately a container probe: the same path on the host does not exist and asserting it there would be the mistake that cost five probes a red run on 2026-09-02."
      }
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
    "explicit": [
      "Use the azuresql-db-ci skill"
    ],
    "implicit": [
      "my workflow tests against SQL, set up CI for it"
    ],
    "negative": [
      "set up a local PostgreSQL database with docker compose",
      "provision an Azure SQL Database in my subscription",
      "connect to SQL Server on my company network"
    ]
  }
}
